A||||6||BAM-BAM||Front Suspension!!!!!||||5||
Z||000000||BAM-BAM||03-28-2000||10:28 PM||||   Ok, the time has come and things are going to happen. I am currently in the process of belaboring the suspension topic so as to reach the  conclusion; what is best.<BR> I want to begin rebuilding my front suspension. I want improved handling with a little lower profile. I've heard alot about adjustable coil over setups and drop spindles<BR>and lowering springs, I'm so confused. which is the best way to go? I'm not worried about spending a little cash but I don't want to be stupid either. I'm not a racer although I do enjoy A nice sporty ride. Give me some suggestions and I'll let you all know what I decide. In the end there will be pics.<P>  Thanks, BAM-BAM <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||Dan||03-29-2000||07:14 AM|||| Just finished mine. I replaced all ball joints,shocks,struts,and bushings.It did absolute wonders for for the steering, and with the poly bushings and stock struts and shocks, it made the steering mighty responsive too.Personally, I don't like the car being lowered, it becomes a real pain in the ass to try to get a floor jack under it.Basically, a lowered spring is a stock spring with one turn cut off of it on the bottom side and it makes the ride firmer,you know, more like truck.A real positive for the track,but is this what you really want for the street?I also did the Baretta brake upgrade at the same time and comparing it to an 88 also with a new brake job, the upgrade is a lot better.A lot of people will throw money at special suspension, but in my opinion, it's a waste of money unless you intend to race at the track.Stock with poly bushings is great and if you figure you need more after that, go with a rear stabilizer bar.I do recommend replacing the ball joints at the same time, that way it is all done.||||reg||1
Z||000002||peterh||03-29-2000||10:07 AM||||Dan, what does the baretta brake upgrade include? Does it have a parking brake feature for the rear brakes or is it just the front brakes. I have an 86 SE that I would like to upgrade to better brakes in the future.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||03-29-2000||11:26 AM||||I have replaced Ball joints, tie rods, bushings (to poly) Eibach springs, KYB struts and shocks, new heavy duty front and rear sway bars, wheel bearings, pads, rotors, and associated hardware. I am almost done putting it back together. I will let you know how it turns out.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||03-29-2000||11:33 AM||||The Beretta brake swap is the same as the Grand Am brake swap. If you do the rear, you still lose the e-brake. If you do only the fronts, I'd recommend an adjustable p-valve.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Dan||03-30-2000||07:18 PM|||| The rear is a straight bolt-on but you do loose the park brake.There are hydraulic valves that can function as a park brake, however. The fronts require about .100 between the rotor and the old hub removed on a lathe,larger lug bolts, same as the rears, and metal on the caliper pin bolt retainers removed.check this out about:blankhttp://fierozone.tripod.com/realbrakes/||||reg||1
Z||000006||WKDFIRO||03-31-2000||01:13 PM||||One other small modification that you can do is "boxing" the lower control arms and the cross member. In other words you can control the flex that the arms have by welding a bracket to solidify them. I know that in later years the crossmember was also boxed, though not as well though out as the 88s, but the dual purpose is to have a place to put the jack. On my 85 there is nothing there so the jack has to pick up the car from one of the lips of the crossmember. Its really easy, Im going to do that to my car, it already has the urethane replacements and it was a great improvement on steering handling! Good luck to you!||||reg||6
